WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:19.373
The winter was miserable. Sara was sent outside in the worst weather.

2
00:00:19.374 --> 00:00:23.551
There were days when she had to walk through snow.

3
00:00:23.552 --> 00:00:28.490
There were worse days when the snow melted into muddy slush.

4
00:00:28.491 --> 00:00:34.905
Those were days when the windows of the Large family's house looked especially cozy.

5
00:00:34.906 --> 00:00:40.980
The study where Mr. Carrisford sat glowed with warmth and color too.

6
00:00:40.981 --> 00:00:45.515
Meanwhile Sara's attic was too dreary for words.

7
00:00:45.516 --> 00:00:51.274
Gray clouds hung low over the skylight. There were no sunsets.

8
00:00:51.275 --> 00:00:57.003
The servants in the kitchen were miserable too, and that made them crueler than ever.

9
00:00:57.004 --> 00:01:00.986
They treated poor Becky like a slave.

10
00:01:00.987 --> 00:01:05.885
One cold, rainy day Sara was doing errands.

11
00:01:05.886 --> 00:01:10.024
Her shabby clothes and old shoes were soaked.

12
00:01:10.025 --> 00:01:17.386
For some reason Miss Minchin had chosen to punish her, so the cook didn't give Sara any lunch.

13
00:01:17.387 --> 00:01:20.575
Still Sara kept doing her errands.

14
00:01:20.576 --> 00:01:24.137
She decided to pretend that things were better.

15
00:01:24.138 --> 00:01:30.165
"I'll pretend I'm wearing warm, dry clothes," she said to herself.

16
00:01:30.166 --> 00:01:34.043
"I'll pretend I have good shoes and an umbrella.

17
00:01:34.044 --> 00:01:38.108
And when I'm near a bakery, I'll find some money.

18
00:01:38.109 --> 00:01:44.325
I'll go into that shop and buy six hot buns. Then I'll eat them all up."

19
00:01:44.326 --> 00:01:49.159
Some very odd things happen in this world sometimes.

20
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:54.792
Just then Sara had to cross the street. The mud was deep and cold.

21
00:01:54.793 --> 00:01:58.447
She walked carefully, looking at the ground.

22
00:01:58.448 --> 00:02:03.201
Just as she reached the sidewalk, she spied a silver coin.

23
00:02:03.202 --> 00:02:07.544
Sara picked it up. Then she looked around.

24
00:02:07.545 --> 00:02:10.557
She was right in front of a bakery!

25
00:02:10.558 --> 00:02:16.452
A cheerful stout woman was putting a tray of freshly baked buns in the window.

26
00:02:16.453 --> 00:02:22.324
They were large, plump, shiny buns with currants in them.

27
00:02:22.325 --> 00:02:30.365
Sara could smell the warm buns. "Oh," she sighed.

28
00:02:30.366 --> 00:02:36.724
"Those buns look delicious. With this coin I could buy four of them.

29
00:02:36.725 --> 00:02:41.104
But first I must ask the baker if she lost any money."

30
00:02:41.105 --> 00:02:45.738
Sara was about to enter the bakery when she stopped.

31
00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:52.625
A small, sad figure sat nearby. It was a beggar girl with big hollow eyes.

32
00:02:52.626 --> 00:02:56.618
Her hair was tangled; her face was dirty.

33
00:02:56.619 --> 00:03:01.583
She was wrapped in rags and had no shoes.

34
00:03:01.584 --> 00:03:05.392
Sara looked at the girl's red muddy feet.

35
00:03:05.393 --> 00:03:10.548
"Princesses always help poor people," she reminded herself.

36
00:03:10.549 --> 00:03:13.905
"That girl looks hungrier than I am."

37
00:03:13.906 --> 00:03:18.306
Then she said to the girl, "Are you hungry?"

38
00:03:18.307 --> 00:03:24.008
"Very hungry, miss. I've had nothing to eat all day."

39
00:03:24.009 --> 00:03:29.276
"Wait here," Sara answered. "I'll be right back."

40
00:03:29.277 --> 00:03:34.234
Sara went into the bakery. It smelled warm and delicious.

41
00:03:34.235 --> 00:03:38.815
The woman was about to put more hot buns in the window.

42
00:03:38.816 --> 00:03:43.128
"Excuse me," Sara said to the baker.

43
00:03:43.129 --> 00:03:47.841
"Did you lose this coin? I found it on the sidewalk."

44
00:03:47.842 --> 00:03:55.418
"No," the woman said. "It was probably there for days. Keep it."

45
00:03:55.419 --> 00:04:01.078
"Then I'd like to buy four buns," Sara answered.

46
00:04:01.079 --> 00:04:07.745
The woman went to the window and put six buns in a bag.

47
00:04:07.746 --> 00:04:12.007
"I can only pay for four," said Sara.

48
00:04:12.008 --> 00:04:15.692
"That's all right," the baker said.

49
00:04:15.693 --> 00:04:20.784
"I bet you can eat six. Aren't you hungry?"

50
00:04:20.785 --> 00:04:28.449
"Yes, very hungry." Tears sprang to Sara's eyes at the woman's kindness.

51
00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:35.157
"Thank you very much," she whispered.

52
00:04:35.158 --> 00:04:41.560
She was about to tell the woman about the girl outside who looked even hungrier than Sara felt.

53
00:04:41.561 --> 00:04:45.506
But just then several customers entered the shop.

54
00:04:45.507 --> 00:04:49.351
So Sara thanked the woman again and left the shop.

55
00:04:49.352 --> 00:04:53.589
The buns were already warming her hands.

56
00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:58.974
She put one in the beggar girl's lap. "Here. Eat this."

57
00:04:58.975 --> 00:05:06.932
"Oh my!" the girl exclaimed as she gobbled up the bun. "This is wonderful!"

58
00:05:06.933 --> 00:05:12.353
Sara handed her three more buns.

59
00:05:12.354 --> 00:05:20.726
"She's starving," Sara thought. "I'm not starving."

60
00:05:20.727 --> 00:05:25.640
And with a trembling hand, she gave the girl a fifth bun.

61
00:05:25.641 --> 00:05:29.725
The beggar girl was still eating when Sara turned away.

62
00:05:29.726 --> 00:05:34.457
Sara nibbled at the sixth bun, trying to make it last.

63
00:05:34.458 --> 00:05:37.616
It was dark when Sara reached her street.

64
00:05:37.617 --> 00:05:42.028
In all the houses, the lights were on.

65
00:05:42.029 --> 00:05:45.990
Sara stopped outside the Large family's house.

66
00:05:45.991 --> 00:05:52.772
She could see the children gathered around Mr. Large, and a carriage was waiting outside the door.

67
00:05:52.773 --> 00:05:56.922
A big suitcase was strapped to the roof.

68
00:05:56.923 --> 00:06:01.556
Sara stepped into the shadows as Mr. Large left the house.

69
00:06:01.557 --> 00:06:04.860
She didn't want anyone to see her.

70
00:06:04.861 --> 00:06:11.303
"Good-bye, Papa!" shouted his children. "We'll miss you!"

71
00:06:11.304 --> 00:06:15.752
"I wish I didn't have to go away," he said.

72
00:06:15.753 --> 00:06:21.447
"I hope you find the little girl," said one of Mr. Large's sons.

73
00:06:21.448 --> 00:06:27.322
As she hurried away, Sara wondered whom Mr. Large was looking for.

74
00:06:27.323 --> 00:06:32.569
Meanwhile Mr. Large's carriage headed for the station.

75
00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:35.776
The train would take him to Moscow.

76
00:06:35.777 --> 00:06:46.983
That's where he hoped to find the little lost daughter of Captain Crewe.
